```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: leaf_router_0_1
diagnosis_text:
The fault is a completely missing BGP configuration on leaf_router_0_1. The
device's FRR running config contains no "router bgp" stanza — only the bare
FRR framework defaults. As a result, leaf_router_0_1 never establishes eBGP
sessions with its two uplinks: spine_router_0_0 (which expects a peer in
AS 65201 at 172.16.0.7) and spine_router_0_1 (which expects AS 65201 at
172.16.0.11). Because no sessions come up, leaf_router_0_1 never originates
or propagates the 10.0.1.0/24 prefix. Every router above it in the CLOS
fabric — both spines and the super-spine — has a route for 10.0.0.0/24
(received from leaf_router_0_0 / AS 65200) but no route whatsoever for
10.0.1.0/24. When pc_0_0 sends a packet to 10.0.1.2, leaf_router_0_0 cannot
find a next-hop and immediately returns an ICMP "Destination Net Unreachable",
which is exactly what the ping trace shows. The team's claim about pc_0_1's
NIC or cabling is incorrect: pc_0_1's eth0 is UP/LOWER_UP with the correct
address (10.0.1.2/24) and zero errors.

Root cause: leaf_router_0_1's FRR BGP daemon was never configured (the entire
"router bgp 65201" block is absent from its running config).

Corrective fix: Apply a BGP configuration to leaf_router_0_1 — router bgp
65201, router-id 172.16.0.7, eBGP neighbors 172.16.0.6 (remote-as 65100) and
172.16.0.10 (remote-as 65101), and advertise network 10.0.1.0/24 in the IPv4
unicast address family. No hardware intervention is needed anywhere.
=== END ===
